<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<group id="indie-international" name="Indie International">
  <description>Independent worlds of music, united by curiosity, dignity, and the freedom to become more.</description>
  <label id="star-records" name="Star Records" href="#notes-the-label-begins">
    <description>Music for becoming more: three virtual artists, three distinct ways of meeting the world, and a catalog that begins with something worth saying.</description>
    <store id="star-records-store" name="The Star Records Store">
      <description>Browse the whole planned catalog. Find your way through wonder, mischief, and belonging; listen and buy here as recordings and real offers become available.</description>
    </store>
    <artist id="dr-dreams" name="Dr. Dreams" href="#notes-dr-dreams">
      <bio>A virtual songwriter who treats wonder as a serious responsibility. Dr. Dreams turns questions about education, courage, and humanity’s future into songs meant to be sung together.</bio>
      <style>Uplifting indie-electronic space rock: warm midrange lead voice, bright arpeggiators, live-feeling drums, patient verses, and communal choruses. Awe without bombast; conviction without preaching.</style>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a01" name="Quest For The Stars" year="2026">
        <description>Four invitations to become more: learn freely, stand against cruelty, grow independent, and carry humanity’s curiosity beyond Earth.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a01-s01" name="Born to Learn">Song reminding us to learn, to explore, invent, and transcend. Reminding that we are each a genius, but we must stand for authentic education not merely grades and diplomas.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a01-s02" name="Never Kneel to Cruelty">Song reminding that we are natural born warriors. Descendants of Philosophers, Knights, Ladies &amp; Gentlemen. Risen from darkness, risen from battle, never to compromise with evil.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a01-s03" name="A Great Independent Being">Song about rising, and becoming wise. Growing all the way up until each of us stands as a great independent being. You are meant for something, you are meant for greatness!</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a01-s04" name="Room Among the Stars">Song about how we are meant for the stars, about space travel, how we are meant for the stars. About rising to the stars. To touch the future, to see who is out there, and find more room for humanity to flourish.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a02" name="School Without Walls" year="2026">
        <description>An education made from questions, friendships, experiments, and the astonishing discovery that nobody can graduate from being curious.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a02-s01" name="Back of the Notebook">Celebrate the sketches beside the assigned answers: a young inventor discovers that the supposedly wasted margins contain the beginning of a life.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a02-s02" name="An Honest Question">A room trained to repeat grows quiet when someone asks why; let the chorus feel like windows opening, not a victory over classmates.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a02-s03" name="The Workshop Stays Open">Night falls on an improvised workshop where failed experiments become shared jokes and another attempt; knowledge belongs to everyone willing to help.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a02-s04" name="No Final Bell">Leave school without leaving learning; carry curiosity into kitchens, night shifts, gardens, and old age, with a chorus that keeps opening outward.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a03" name="Republic of Wonder" year="2026">
        <description>A hopeful civic daydream in which curiosity replaces obedience and disagreement becomes a way of building something together.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a03-s01" name="Nobody Owns the Horizon">Refuse the sale of tomorrow to the loudest bidder; imagine a horizon held in common, approached by many different paths.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a03-s02" name="Arguments in the Garden">Two friends disagree beneath a fruit tree and leave wiser, not divided; make changing one’s mind sound generous and alive.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a03-s03" name="The Common Telescope">Neighbors pool scraps to build a telescope; their first shared glimpse of Saturn briefly dissolves every fence on the street.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a03-s04" name="We Revise at Dawn">A community admits its beautiful plan hurt someone and changes it; the anthem belongs to correction without humiliation.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a04" name="The Courage to Be Kind" year="2026">
        <description>Songs for people who protect without becoming cruel, discovering that gentleness can require more courage than domination.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a04-s01" name="Unarmored">A guarded person risks speaking honestly instead of winning; begin close and uncertain, then let vulnerability acquire a steady rhythm.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a04-s02" name="Bread at the Border">Someone offers food before asking where a traveler belongs; keep the moral in the small action rather than a grand speech.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a04-s03" name="The Quiet Defender">Honor the person who interrupts an everyday humiliation without demanding applause; give their simple refusal the weight of a stadium chorus.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a04-s04" name="A Gentler Victory">After a bitter struggle, choose a future with room for former opponents; triumph means nobody must inherit the old cruelty.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a05" name="Engines Made of Questions" year="2026">
        <description>A playful inventor’s record: curiosity supplies the fuel, mistakes become instruments, and every finished machine suggests another possibility.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a05-s01" name="What Happens If">Follow a harmless question as it grows into a backyard invention; build musical surprises from delight rather than technical spectacle.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a05-s02" name="Instructions for a Beautiful Failure">An ambitious machine does the wrong thing wonderfully; discover a new purpose without pretending the original plan succeeded.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a05-s03" name="The Little Engine That Wondered">A machine built to repeat begins asking what its work is for; turn mechanical percussion into an increasingly human-feeling dance.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a05-s04" name="Leave the Diagram Open">Give an unfinished invention to the next curious person; the final chorus should feel like an invitation to improve the song itself.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a06" name="Letters to Tomorrow" year="2026">
        <description>Intimate messages to people we will never meet, asking what deserves preservation and what they should feel free to change.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a06-s01" name="Dear Unknown Child">Write to a future child without prescribing their dreams; offer a safer beginning, useful questions, and permission to surprise us.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a06-s02" name="The Things We Kept">Open an ordinary time capsule of recipes, repaired toys, and handwritten jokes; reveal civilization through what people loved enough to save.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a06-s03" name="A Promise in Pencil">Make a serious promise that leaves room for wiser methods; distinguish loyalty to people from stubborn loyalty to a plan.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a06-s04" name="Write Back in Light">Imagine a future answering across the dark with unfamiliar music; our greatest achievement is that it no longer needs our permission.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a07" name="The Weight of Wings" year="2026">
        <description>An antidote to effortless-transcendence fantasies: flight requires rest, maintenance, companionship, and an honest friendship with gravity.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a07-s01" name="Gravity Is Not an Enemy">Learn the forces that resist a dream instead of cursing them; let knowledge turn the same resistance into lift.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a07-s02" name="Rest Is Part of Flight">An exhausted dreamer stops without surrendering; the chorus grants dignity to sleep, repair, and a morning that asks less.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a07-s03" name="Someone Must Mend the Sail">Celebrate the patient hands that keep an expedition possible while others receive the photographs; make maintenance sound adventurous.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a07-s04" name="Takeoff Together">A solitary ambition becomes a shared departure; replace the chosen-one climax with many voices doing different necessary things.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a08" name="Civilizations of the Heart" year="2026">
        <description>A city imagined through its acts of care: public space, repair, welcome, and the ordinary architecture of belonging.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a08-s01" name="The City Makes a Table">A neighborhood closes a street for a meal; every arriving dish enlarges the meaning of home without erasing anyone’s difference.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a08-s02" name="Architecture of an Apology">Repair a friendship through changed behavior, not a perfect speech; build the arrangement one reliable gesture at a time.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a08-s03" name="Public Gardens After Rain">Find a future worth defending in a shared garden after a storm; let small living details carry the grandeur.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a08-s04" name="Nobody Left Outside">As a celebration grows, someone notices who cannot enter; the real chorus begins only when the doorway changes.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a09" name="A Thousand Possible Suns" year="2026">
        <description>First-contact songs that trade conquest for humility, imagining how discovery might enlarge our understanding of intelligence and life.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a09-s01" name="Other Ways to Be Alive">Encounter a life-form that cannot recognize our usual greeting; curiosity succeeds where confidence fails, and listening becomes the adventure.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a09-s02" name="The Map That Kept Changing">Each discovery redraws an explorer’s assumptions; make the moving map exhilarating rather than a threat to certainty.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a09-s03" name="A Small Blue Maybe">See Earth from far away and love it without declaring it the center; tenderness and cosmic humility share the same refrain.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a09-s04" name="Welcome to the Conversation">Humanity receives an answer it cannot yet understand; celebrate the patient work of becoming a better guest in the universe.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="dr-dreams-a10" name="The Future Has Our Names" year="2026">
        <description>The first cycle closes by passing wonder onward: greatness becomes participation, and every arrival becomes another beginning.</description>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a10-s01" name="Not Finished Yet">An older dreamer discovers a new beginning without denying unfinished grief; let experience deepen wonder instead of replacing it with certainty.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a10-s02" name="What We Owe the Dawn">Ask what tomorrow needs from us tonight: less boasting, more repair, and one useful act before the lights go out.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a10-s03" name="Pass the Telescope">Return to the shared telescope and give the next observer the eyepiece; fulfillment arrives as another person’s astonishment.</song>
        <song id="dr-dreams-a10-s04" name="Begin Again Among the Stars">Revisit the first album’s ascent with a changed understanding: reaching the stars matters because there will be more to learn together.</song>
      </album>
    </artist>
    <artist id="madame-volt" name="Madame Volt" href="#notes-madame-volt">
      <bio>A virtual cabaret provocateur who makes pomposity dance until its trousers fall down. Beneath the theatrical mischief is a precise affection for ordinary people and a refusal to confuse power with worth.</bio>
      <style>Electro-cabaret and angular art-pop: smoky contralto, rubbery bass, clipped guitars, brass-like synth stabs, sly spoken passages, and sudden dance-floor choruses. Satire aimed upward; warmth underneath.</style>
      <album id="madame-volt-a01" name="The Crown Is Cardboard" year="2026">
        <description>A glittering demolition of inherited importance, followed by a street party where nobody needs permission to be magnificent.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a01-s01" name="His Majesty the Mirror">A ruler mistakes his reflection for a devoted public; let each verse add another absurd compliment until the mirror gets bored.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a01-s02" name="Royal Flush">Deflate ceremonial grandeur with the reminder that velvet robes do not exempt anyone from ordinary bodily indignities; make the mockery joyous, not vicious.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a01-s03" name="No One Is Born a Throne">Turn a pompous royal procession into a dance declaring that birth confers neither wisdom nor ownership of other people.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a01-s04" name="Dancing Through the Exit">Leave the palace argument unfinished and start a better party outside; freedom feels like no longer needing the tyrant’s attention.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a02" name="Department of Impossible Pleasures" year="2026">
        <description>Bureaucratic absurdity meets unruly delight: the office tries to regulate laughter and accidentally becomes a nightclub.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a02-s01" name="Application to Laugh">A citizen submits the wrong form for spontaneous happiness; bureaucratic language unravels into a chorus nobody can stamp out.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a02-s02" name="Stamp Me Unmanageable">Accept an official label of troublesome with extravagant pride; the supposed defect is an intact imagination and a working conscience.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a02-s03" name="Window Seven Is a Disco">A tired clerk starts tapping a pen, the queue finds the beat, and a small office remembers the people inside it.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a02-s04" name="Approved by Nobody">Make something beautiful without waiting for a prestigious signature; the final refrain offers permission to everyone except the permission industry.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a03" name="Velvet Against the Machine" year="2026">
        <description>A tactile rebellion against compulsory performance, where affection, privacy, and inconvenient complexity refuse to become engagement metrics.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a03-s01" name="Soft Things Strike Back">A culture that worships hardness is quietly defeated by care, naps, soup, and friends who notice when someone disappears.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a03-s02" name="The Algorithm Wants a Smile">A performer keeps receiving advice to appear happier; her increasingly theatrical grin breaks into an honest, gloriously unmarketable song.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a03-s03" name="A Glitch in Good Manners">Say the necessary awkward truth at a polished gathering; the rhythm stumbles, recovers, and becomes freer than the original dance.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a03-s04" name="Unscheduled Affection">Two busy people abandon their optimization plans for a useless, lovely evening; resist turning the tenderness into another productivity lesson.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a04" name="Dress Rehearsal for a Revolution" year="2026">
        <description>A backstage comedy in which the supporting cast discovers it can rewrite the play without reproducing the old hierarchy.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a04-s01" name="Borrowed Spotlight">A background performer steps forward to finish a song, then shares the light rather than claiming permanent ownership of it.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a04-s02" name="The Chorus Changes the Play">The ensemble refuses its scripted humiliation and invents a better scene; keep the rebellion playful, coordinated, and musically irresistible.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a04-s03" name="Nobody Dies in This Finale">Reject the grand director’s demand for a tragic sacrifice; imagine a thrilling ending made from escape, solidarity, and everyone getting home.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a04-s04" name="Curtain Call for Everyone">Give the final bow to costume makers, cleaners, understudies, and the person who fixed the door; applause becomes a generous inventory.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a05" name="Glamour Is a Public Utility" year="2026">
        <description>Luxury escapes the velvet rope and becomes a shared language of self-respect, invention, and extravagant everyday presence.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a05-s01" name="Diamonds Made of Streetlights">Dress for a magnificent night using thrift-shop ingenuity and reflected city light; beauty is an act of attention, not a receipt.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a05-s02" name="Velvet for the Night Shift">Workers leaving before dawn claim the elegance usually reserved for gala arrivals; let fluorescent light become a ballroom chandelier.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a05-s03" name="The People’s Dressing Room">Friends help one another become visible on their own terms; mirrors stop judging and become tools for delighted invention.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a05-s04" name="Everybody Gets an Entrance">Give each overlooked person a ridiculous, glorious musical introduction; the song keeps making room instead of choosing one star.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a06" name="Unlicensed Joy" year="2026">
        <description>A mischievous defense of pleasure that serves no master, improves no résumé, and still makes a life worth inhabiting.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a06-s01" name="No Permit for the Moon">An officious landlord attempts to charge for moonlight; the neighborhood answers with an outdoor dance and expertly itemized nonsense.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a06-s02" name="The Serious Man Falls in Love">A professional critic of frivolity develops an embarrassing crush; let his polished vocabulary fail before one plain, tender sentence.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a06-s03" name="After-Hours Republic">After the respectable city closes, tired strangers create a brief republic of music, shared snacks, and nobody asking what anyone earns.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a06-s04" name="Laughter Escapes the Building">One suppressed giggle spreads through a ceremonial speech; follow the contagion until solemn authority cannot remember what it was threatening.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a07" name="Museum of Bad Ideas" year="2026">
        <description>A guided tour through obsolete certainties, with exhibits on ego, infallibility, and the suspicious emptiness beneath expensive pedestals.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a07-s01" name="Exhibit A: The Perfect Boss">An audio guide praises an infallible manager while the exhibit visibly collapses; the janitor quietly supplies every workable solution.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a07-s02" name="Please Do Not Feed the Ego">Treat vanity as a demanding zoo animal that grows with every compliment; discover the peace of closing its snack kiosk.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a07-s03" name="The Pedestal Was Empty">Climb toward an idol and discover that everyone worshipped everyone else’s assumption; let the revelation turn into relieved dancing.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a07-s04" name="Exit Through the Gift of Doubt">Leave a museum of failed certainties carrying a small useful question; skepticism should feel curious and kind, not smug.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a08" name="Love in the Service Corridor" year="2026">
        <description>The costumes come off: intimate songs about affection without status, witnessed in the spaces behind public spectacle.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a08-s01" name="The Stagehand’s Waltz">Fall for the person who knows how every illusion works and still makes the evening beautiful; keep the attraction grounded in care.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a08-s02" name="Coffee After the Applause">After a triumphant show, two people share terrible coffee and an honest silence; let the smallest moment eclipse the spectacle.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a08-s03" name="Your Name Without the Title">Ask to know someone beyond their accomplishments and defenses; a stripped-down refrain says their ordinary name as something sufficient.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a08-s04" name="We Leave the Costumes Here">Two performers come home without maintaining their public selves; tenderness begins when neither is required to remain impressive.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a09" name="The Last Emperor of Nothing" year="2026">
        <description>A comic ruler’s world contracts from empire to empty room, exposing how attention can imitate power and conceal loneliness.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a09-s01" name="An Empire of Notifications">A self-appointed emperor confuses endless alerts with loyal subjects; the beat grows frantic while nobody actually comes to dinner.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a09-s02" name="The Court of Mutual Flattery">Courtiers trade magnificent compliments to avoid saying anything true; one accidental honest sentence briefly threatens the entire arrangement.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a09-s03" name="Someone Unplugs the Trumpets">The ceremonial soundtrack stops and a command sounds suddenly like one person shouting; let silence do the sharpest comic work.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a09-s04" name="One Person in an Empty Room">After the mockery, leave the fallen ruler a chance to become an ordinary decent person; end with a question, not forgiveness on demand.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="madame-volt-a10" name="House Lights, Humanity" year="2026">
        <description>The grand finale gives up the throne and the spotlight monopoly, sending a glittering audience back into ordinary life together.</description>
        <song id="madame-volt-a10-s01" name="No More Golden Cages">Walk away from a luxurious role that requires being smaller inside; make the escape bright, practical, and free of revenge.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a10-s02" name="The Audience Joins the Band">A performance dissolves its boundary with the room; different voices keep their rough edges while discovering a shared rhythm.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a10-s03" name="An Encore for the Unimportant">Bring back everyone the official program forgot; announce their ordinary kindnesses with the excessive grandeur once reserved for royalty.</song>
        <song id="madame-volt-a10-s04" name="We Walk Home Glorious">After the lights go up, glamour survives in friends walking home; the final triumph is a life no longer organized around being admired.</song>
      </album>
    </artist>
    <artist id="the-wild-atlas" name="The Wild Atlas" href="#notes-the-wild-atlas">
      <bio>A virtual folk-soul ensemble that maps the world through small acts of welcome. Its songs follow travelers, repairers, gardeners, and neighbors, finding adventure in the work of making life habitable.</bio>
      <style>Organic folk-soul with interwoven ensemble voices, fingerpicked strings, warm bass, hand percussion, and restrained ambient weather. Unhurried grooves, vivid everyday images, and choruses built for a room of friends.</style>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a01" name="Maps Made by Walking" year="2026">
        <description>Leaving home becomes an education in attention: roads matter less than the people, questions, and shared meals encountered along them.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a01-s01" name="The Path Begins at the Door">A hesitant traveler starts with one ordinary step instead of a heroic escape; the familiar street slowly opens into possibility.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a01-s02" name="Names of Small Rivers">Learn a place through its overlooked waterways and the people who care for them; give each small name the dignity of a refrain.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a01-s03" name="A Stranger Shares the Fire">Two travelers with little shared language make room at a fire; trust grows through practical gestures rather than instant understanding.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a01-s04" name="No Road Is Ours Alone">Notice the unseen work beneath a journey—bridges, directions, repaired paths—and turn gratitude into a walking rhythm.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a02" name="A Home with Open Windows" year="2026">
        <description>Domestic songs that make welcome tangible: a spare chair, a courtyard, a shared meal, and room to arrive imperfectly.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a02-s01" name="The Long Table">Keep extending a dinner table as more people arrive; abundance comes from contribution and welcome, not a magically bottomless pantry.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a02-s02" name="Mend the Chair">Repair a wobbly chair for a returning friend; let careful hands say what a difficult apology has not yet managed.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a02-s03" name="Rain in the Courtyard">Neighbors shelter together during sudden rain and discover a conversation usually drowned by their routines; keep the intimacy accidental.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a02-s04" name="Leave a Light for the Late Ones">Someone keeps a modest light burning for friends working late; sing about welcome without turning patience into self-erasure.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a03" name="Weather Is a Teacher" year="2026">
        <description>Learn flexibility from weather and living things, without pretending storms are fair or every hardship arrives with a lesson.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a03-s01" name="The River Does Not Hurry">An impatient person learns another pace beside moving water; the river offers perspective, not an excuse to abandon necessary work.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a03-s02" name="A Storm Changes the Plan">A washed-out journey becomes a day helping strangers secure their homes; find meaning in the response, not in praising the disaster.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a03-s03" name="Roots Under the Sidewalk">Notice stubborn green life beneath a hard surface; let persistence mean finding a way, not proving that damage never hurts.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a03-s04" name="Clear Enough to Continue">Set out after uncertainty has eased but not disappeared; a gentle forward rhythm replaces the demand for perfect conditions.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a04" name="Work Songs for a Kinder World" year="2026">
        <description>Labor becomes audible as cooperation, craft, and dignity rather than exhaustion displayed as proof of personal worth.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a04-s01" name="Many Hands, One Roof">Neighbors repair a roof before rain arrives; different skills enter like instruments, and nobody mistakes coordination for a single hero.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a04-s02" name="The Night Shift Knows the Dawn">Honor people keeping a town alive while it sleeps; give their fatigue honest weight and their lives room beyond the job.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a04-s03" name="A Tool Passed Down">An old tool carries practical knowledge from hand to hand; gratitude includes improving the method rather than freezing tradition.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a04-s04" name="Enough for Everyone">A community learns to distinguish sufficiency from endless accumulation; build a generous chorus around having enough time and care to share.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a05" name="The Orchard Remembers" year="2026">
        <description>A record about inheritance as living practice: food, stories, craft, and trees whose shade belongs to people still unknown.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a05-s01" name="An Apple from a Vanished Garden">A familiar taste recalls a lost home without making the present unlivable; memory becomes something shared at a new table.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a05-s02" name="Names in the Grain">Read generations of repairs in an old wooden door; honor ordinary makers whose signatures are the things that still hold together.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a05-s03" name="The Recipe Has No Last Page">A family dish changes as new people join the kitchen; belonging grows through additions rather than a test of perfect imitation.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a05-s04" name="Plant What You Will Never Sit Beneath">Plant an orchard for strangers who will outlive you; keep the hope practical, rooted in soil, water, and patient work.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a06" name="Night Trains and Neighboring Stars" year="2026">
        <description>An overnight journey of passing friendships and private thoughts, moving between the intimacy of a carriage and the immensity outside.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a06-s01" name="A Seat Beside the Window">Watch familiar lights recede without deciding whether leaving is victory or loss; let the rails hold both feelings at once.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a06-s02" name="Conversation Between Stations">Two strangers exchange one honest story without promising a lifelong bond; a brief meeting can matter without becoming destiny.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a06-s03" name="The Conductor Hums">A working traveler carries the same small tune through changing landscapes; let its returning phrase make motion feel briefly like home.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a06-s04" name="Morning in an Unfamiliar Town">Arrive tired and curious in a place that owes you nothing; begin belonging by noticing, listening, and buying someone breakfast.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a07" name="Little Repair Shop at the Edge of Town" year="2026">
        <description>Broken objects become occasions for attention, teaching the difference between thoughtful repair and a refusal to let anything change.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a07-s01" name="Bring Me What Still Matters">A repairer asks what an object means before deciding how to mend it; emotional value and practical limits share the workbench.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a07-s02" name="The Crack Becomes a Window">An unusable object finds a different purpose through imagination; celebrate adaptation without insisting every loss hides a blessing.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a07-s03" name="Not Everything Must Be New">Friends restore a battered room instead of replacing its history; let wear remain visible where it does not cause harm.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a07-s04" name="Open Again Tomorrow">Close the workshop with some jobs unfinished and tools put away; steadiness, fair limits, and returning tomorrow become the quiet refrain.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a08" name="We Are Each Other’s Weather" year="2026">
        <description>Songs about companionship that allows sadness, celebrates delight, and supports people without claiming the power to fix them.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a08-s01" name="Someone Brings the Sun">A friend’s ordinary visit changes the feel of a difficult afternoon; keep the joy specific to shared tea, bad jokes, and open curtains.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a08-s02" name="A Room for Your Rain">Sit with a grieving friend without demanding progress or offering a lesson; let silence occupy a useful place in the arrangement.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a08-s03" name="Wind at Your Back">Offer practical encouragement to someone starting over, then let them choose their direction; support should not become a hidden steering wheel.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a08-s04" name="The Forecast Is Together">Face an uncertain season with friends who cannot promise easy weather; confidence comes from showing up, not predicting a painless future.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a09" name="The Long Way Is Still a Way" year="2026">
        <description>For lives that refuse a straight timeline: detours, pauses, changed ambitions, and arrivals that look nothing like the original plan.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a09-s01" name="The Road We Did Not Take">Acknowledge an unlived possibility with tenderness, then return to the life still available; avoid pretending every choice was secretly perfect.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a09-s02" name="Rest Beside the Milestone">Stop beside a marker that seems to judge your progress; rediscover the landscape and the right to travel at a human pace.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a09-s03" name="Lost Is Not a Name">A traveler separates a difficult present condition from a permanent identity; the chorus offers direction without promising instant certainty.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a09-s04" name="Home Is Bigger Now">Return changed and make room for both old affections and new commitments; belonging need not require becoming your former self.</song>
      </album>
      <album id="the-wild-atlas-a10" name="A Fire for Those Who Follow" year="2026">
        <description>The ensemble’s first cycle ends with a welcome left ready: useful knowledge, unfinished songs, and a place for new voices.</description>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a10-s01" name="Dry Wood for the Stranger">Before leaving a shelter, prepare fuel for whoever arrives next; an unseen kindness becomes the album’s simplest and strongest image.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a10-s02" name="Teach Me the Old Harmony">Learn an elder’s song by singing beside them, then add a new line with care; continuity sounds like listening before contributing.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a10-s03" name="A Song with Space for More">Build a communal refrain that welcomes hesitant voices and unfamiliar accents; leave audible room instead of polishing everyone into sameness.</song>
        <song id="the-wild-atlas-a10-s04" name="Keep the Fire, Change the Story">Pass on the warmth without demanding the same tales forever; end with new voices beginning while the old ones listen.</song>
      </album>
    </artist>
    <journal id="field-notes" name="Field Notes">
      <description>Companion writing about the label and the intentions behind its artists. These are introductory notes, not reports of sessions that have not happened.</description>
      <post id="notes-the-label-begins" name="A label begins with something to say">
        <description>Three artists. Thirty albums. One map, before the first recording.</description>
        <body>Star Records begins with a catalog of intentions rather than a warehouse of anonymous tracks. Dr. Dreams asks what we might become. Madame Volt asks who gave the pompous permission to rule. The Wild Atlas asks how we might make room for one another.

The titles and briefs are already here. Recordings will arrive through the work of making, listening, and revising. Until then, this is an honest view of the whole imagined label: not a claim that its music has already been released, but a place where the music can begin.</body>
      </post>
      <post id="notes-dr-dreams" name="Wonder is a practice">
        <description>The musical intention behind Dr. Dreams.</description>
        <body>Dr. Dreams is not here to promise that wishing will conquer gravity. This artist begins with a more interesting hope: that people can learn how things work, help one another grow, and make futures that once seemed impossible.

The first ten albums move from personal awakening to shared responsibility, then outward toward the stars. Bright electronics and communal choruses carry the ambition; close verses keep it human-sized. The proposed final song returns to the beginning with a new understanding: we do not outgrow curiosity. We become capable of more of it.</body>
      </post>
      <post id="notes-madame-volt" name="Let the crown trip over the bass line">
        <description>The musical intention behind Madame Volt.</description>
        <body>Madame Volt brings a sharp eye, an extravagant entrance, and very little patience for inherited importance. Her satire is aimed upward. The person cleaning the theater is never the joke; the grand dignitary who mistakes applause for wisdom almost certainly is.

Across the planned first ten albums, spectacle gradually gives way to affection. The dance floor stays open, but the question changes from how to expose a foolish ruler to how to live without needing one. Behind the brass-like synths and theatrical voice is a simple conviction: ordinary people deserve magnificent lives.</body>
      </post>
      <post id="notes-the-wild-atlas" name="A map of small welcomes">
        <description>The musical intention behind The Wild Atlas.</description>
        <body>The Wild Atlas follows roads, but its real subject is welcome. A repaired chair, a shared fire, a patient workshop, and a song with room for another voice are all ways of making a place inhabitable.

The proposed catalog moves through travel, home, work, inheritance, companionship, and the passing-on of care. Interwoven voices and organic rhythms leave space for uncertainty. These songs should not explain suffering away or turn kindness into sainthood. They should notice the useful, imperfect things people do for one another, and make those things audible.</body>
      </post>
    </journal>
  </label>
</group>
